# decision Cargo git dependencies use rev or branch

The Cargo adapter owns one entry in `[dependencies]`. Locked wiring writes the full lock commit
as `rev`; floating wiring writes the declared ref as `branch`. Cargo discovers a named crate
anywhere in the Git repository, so an export subdirectory does not become a Cargo `path` key.
Unrelated TOML text and dependency entries remain byte-for-byte unchanged.
If `[dependencies]` is absent, the adapter creates it inside a delimited block; unwire removes
the block as a unit instead of leaving an empty table.

# decision Cargo.lock stays downstream

The adapter does not run `cargo update` while wiring. That command can resolve and rewrite
unrelated packages; the consumer's normal Cargo workflow creates or refreshes `Cargo.lock`.

# test Cargo adapter

- Fixture/golden wiring is idempotent and preserves unrelated entries and comments.
- Drift checks both the normalized Git URL and the locked revision.
- Unwire removes only the exported crate entry.

# decision Refresh prerequisite

`Refresh` checks `cargo` through the shared prerequisite contract before doing any work.
